    {
      "id": "source-text",
      "type": "markdown",
      "markdown": "Figure 3.1: Graph of  $f(x) = \\frac{x^2 - 4}{x - 2}$ \n\n $\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 2^+} f(x) = 4$ \n\nThis can be written mathematically as follows:\n\n$$\\boxed{\\lim_{x \\rightarrow 2} f(x) = 4.}$$\n\nConsider another function  $g(x) = \\frac{x^2 - 5}{x - 2}$  or  $y = \\frac{x^2 - 5}{x - 2}$ .\n\n| $x$    | $g(x) = \\frac{x^2 - 5}{x - 2}$ |\n|--------|--------------------------------|\n| 1.9    | 13.9                           |\n| 1.99   | 103.99                         |\n| 1.999  | 1003.999                       |\n| 1.9999 | 10,003.9999                    |\n\n| $x$    | $y = \\frac{x^2 - 5}{x - 2}$ |\n|--------|-----------------------------|\n| 2.1    | -5.1                        |\n| 2.01   | -95.99                      |\n| 2.001  | -995.999                    |\n| 2.0001 | -9,995.9999                 |"
    },
    {
      "id": "source-correction",
      "type": "markdown",
      "markdown": "**Source comparison note:** The original guide prints $-5.1$ in the row $x=2.1$. That printed entry is inconsistent with the stated function. Substitution gives\n\n$$\ng(2.1)=\\frac{2.1^2-5}{2.1-2}=\\frac{-0.59}{0.1}=-5.9.\n$$\n\nThe original table is preserved above; use $-5.9$ for the calculated value."
    }
  ]
